  "summary": "Distinctive fortified compound architecture of the Somba people characterized by multi-story structures with granaries and living quarters.",
  "description": "Features include two-story mud-brick compounds with flat roofs, granaries on upper levels, and defensive elements like walls and watchtowers. Developed as protection against slave raids while maintaining family living spaces and food storage in a compact, vertical arrangement.",
  "culturalContext": "Tata Somba — Benin and Togo's fortified house-towers (UNESCO-listed Koutammakou landscape for the Batammariba) — stack defense and domesticity in clay: two-to-three story turrets with granary ears, granary-and-living floors, spiked battle-proof walls, and rooftop sleeping platforms reached by interior ladders. Each tata is a family castle; the landscape of 100+ towers against the Atakora hills is West Africa's most striking vernacular skyline, still built by tabarma masons with ceremonial foundations.",
